op)\n- This is Round 0 (pre-flop), no community cards yet\n\n**Hand Strength:**\nPocket Queens is the 3rd best starting hand in Texas Hold'em, only behind pocket Aces and pocket Kings. This is a very strong hand pre-flop with approximately 80% equity against most random hands, and about 50% against hands like A-K.\n\n**Opponent's Action Analysis:**\nMy opponent went all-in pre-flop. This is an aggressive move that could indicate:\n1. A premium hand (A-A, K-K, A-K)\n2. A bluff/semi-bluff trying to steal the blinds\n3. A desperate move if they're behind\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- I'm down 600 chips cumulatively (losing overall)\n- With 13 hands remaining, there's still plenty of time to recover\n- Folding Q-Q pre-flop to an all-in would be extremely tight and likely -EV (negative expected value)\n- Pocket Queens has strong equity against most holdings\n- Even against A-A or K-K (worst case), I still have ~18-20% chance to win\n\n**Options:**\n1. **Fold (-1)**: Extremely weak play. Would waste a premium hand and lose the blinds already invested\n2. **Call/Check (0)**: Not valid here - I need to call the all-in amount\n3. **All-in (-2)**: Match the opponent's bet with my remaining chips\n\nReason:\nPocket Queens is too strong to fold pre-flop against a single opponent, even facing an all-in. This hand has excellent equity against the vast majority of possible holdings. While there's risk my opponent has A-A or K-K, they could also have A-K, J-J, 10-10, or even be bluffing. The mathematical expectation of calling with Q-Q is positive. Folding here would be giving up significant expected value and would be considered a major mistake in poker strategy.\n\nAnswer: -2"}
