n- Position: Pre-flop (no community cards yet)\n- Hand strength: Strong (pocket Jacks)\n- Chips: 19,950 (essentially full stack)\n- I'm ahead in cumulative chips (+19,200)\n- Valid actions: Fold, Check, Raise (minimum 200), or All-in\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n\n1. **Check (0)**: This is cautious but wastes the value of a premium hand. Pocket Jacks should be played aggressively pre-flop to:\n   - Build the pot when I'm likely ahead\n   - Force weaker hands to fold\n   - Charge drawing hands to see the flop\n\n2. **Raise 200 (minimum)**: A small raise shows strength but:\n   - Keeps my opponent in the hand with weaker holdings\n   - Builds a pot while maintaining pot control\n   - Allows me to see how opponent responds\n\n3. **Larger Raise (400-800)**: More aggressive, builds a bigger pot, and puts more pressure on opponent.\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Extremely aggressive but risks everything. With JJ, this is probably too much variance pre-flop, as I'd only get called by better hands (QQ+, AK) typically.\n\n5. **Fold (-1)**: Absolutely not with pocket Jacks.\n\n**Best Strategy:**\nWith pocket Jacks, I should raise to build the pot and take control. A standard raise of 3-4x the big blind (assuming big blind is 100, so 300-400) is appropriate. This protects my hand while still allowing action.\n\n# Reason:\n\nPocket Jacks is a premium hand that should be played aggressively pre-flop. A raise of **400 chips** accomplishes multiple goals:\n- Builds the pot when I'm likely ahead\n- Puts pressure on my opponent\n- Defines their hand strength based on their response\n- Maintains enough chips for post-flop play\n- Standard sizing that doesn't commit my entire stack\n\nThis balanced approach maximizes value while managing risk appropriately for a hand this strong.\n\n# Answer: 400"}
